  geopolitics: "The transmission chain this file has been tracking reversed direction in a single session, and the reversal came from a statement rather than from supply. For a week the market had been pricing a restarted US-Iran negotiation: Trump had authorised Vance to negotiate, the Wall Street Journal reported Iran would allow 25 tankers through the Strait in exchange for easing the blockade on its own ports, and Iran's foreign minister said the offer to reopen the Strait within two days stood if the war ended on all fronts. Crude fell accordingly, to 86.075 on WTI and 92.440 on Brent. Then Trump said the United States does not need a deal with Iran, because it already has their money - hundreds of billions of dollars - and it has their Strait. Brent settled 3.3% higher at 95.82 and now prints 96.035, with WTI at 88.483. Both weekly declines have more than halved, to minus 2.32% and minus 2.58%. The earlier signals are still on the record and still contradictory: Iran's security council secretary Mohsen Rezaei dismissed the talks as fake news, Rubio has called Iran's demands outrageous and said the two sides are not close, Iran has rejected a key US nuclear demand, and attacks on tankers near the Strait continue alongside naval mines and elevated insurance costs. Against that, crude flows through Hormuz and the wider Middle East have in practice recovered toward pre-war levels, OPEC+ output is surging, and US crude inventories built roughly 9 million barrels last week - which is why this is a risk premium returning to a well-supplied market rather than a genuine shortage, and why USOIL is a range rather than a long. The FX consequences run three ways and all three are changes from yesterday. First, the franc. The SNB eased its threat to intervene in September on the explicit judgement that war-induced haven flows had subsided; a collapsed negotiation with the Strait still blocked is exactly the event that brings them back, and a central bank that has stepped back from selling francs is less able to cap the bid. CHF comes up from minus two to minus one and every franc short on the board comes down in conviction. Second, sterling. Bailey's stated condition for the November hike not being the last is that the conflict is not resolved, and it is not resolving; more directly, UK gas at 236.10 pence per therm is plus 11.24% on the week and plus 9.30% on the month, the sharpest energy input in G10. GBP goes back to plus one. The same channel runs through the euro area, where gas at 94.74 euros is plus 10.69% on the week and German gas gained 6.31% in a single session, which is part of why December ECB pricing moved to 94% to 95%. Third, and least obvious, the dollar. The bond selloff that took the US 10-year from 5.00% to 5.127% was attributed to pricier oil keeping inflation sticky as much as to the Fed itself, which means the Iran reversal is now a US rates story and the dollar's break through 103.00 partly rests on it. The reciprocal is the one to watch: a genuine de-escalation would now hit the dollar, sterling, the euro and the franc short all at once, and it is the single event that would require rewriting most of this board.",
